    "finish_elapsed_s": 10.556835185969248,
    "text": "A refrigerator works by moving heat, not by making cold. A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. First, it passes through a compressor that squeezes it, making it hot. Then it travels through coils on the back, where that heat escapes into your kitchen. Next, the refrigerant expands inside the fridge, which makes it very cold. Because cold things absorb heat, the refrigerant soaks up warmth from your food. It then cycles back to the compressor to start over. A thermostat watches the temperature and switches the compressor on and off to keep things just right. So your fridge isn't creating cold—it's carrying heat out!"
  }
